  "notes": "The loudest, most percussive corner of the lane, and the research states the defining parameter as a negative: \"almost no dynamic range by design\". That is authored as a `velocityTiers` override — the only one that matters here — with the ghost tier lifted to 92 so a ghost stroke is still an event rather than a whisper, and `velocityVar` at 0.05. The kick grammar is the other marker: 1, 2.5 and 3.5 is three hits, not two, so `anchors` carries `2&` alongside 1 and `densityPerBar` starts at 3. Snare is layered with both a clap and a rimshot, which is why `rimshotLayer` and `layerClapOffsetMs` are both set, and the crash accents every 4 bars are the `percs` lane. The bass is short punchy hits on the downbeats rather than a sustained sub, so `mirror_kick` with `glideProb` 0 and near-zero anticipation. ⛔ `chords.impliedByRiff` is set as a description: \"static minor with one riff; the riff *is* the hook\". Its chord variety therefore comes from `chordDurationBeats` and three `harmonicRhythm` values, and the `i–VII` family is INFERRED at the lowest weight so the bass has a second reachable root — delete it first if this ever sounds busier than the records. ⚠ `snareRoll.velocityRampRange` is [72,112] rather than this band's usual [52,96]. That is a deliberate, research-driven deviation: a roll that ramps up from 52 contradicts the entry's central claim, and the rest of the band fingerprint (16th subdivisions, even grouping, fills every 2 and 8) is unchanged. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it medium-high and the documentation is song-and-roster level rather than session level. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 44 to 43 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 44 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 1.",
